  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA broad shortwave trough will approach and cross the Upper Ohio\nValley region today. At the same time, a surface cold front is\nexpected to drop slowly south across the region. Showers and\nthunderstorms will be ongoing this morning with the approach of\nthe shortwave. An increase in coverage of showers and storms is\nexpected with building diurnal instability. The approaching\nfront will also be a focus for additional convection later this\nmorning through the early evening. \n\nIssued a Flash Flood Watch from this morning through this\nevening with one hour flash flood guidance ranging from 0.75 to\n1.5 inches for much of our area. HREF probability signals for\nheavy rain are mainly focused across the PBZ forecast area as\nwell. PWATs are progged to range from 1.6 to 1.8 inches though\nmuch of the day, before beginning to diminish tonight. Some\nincrease in flow aloft should result in a storm motion of 25-30\nmph, though the potential for training, especially in the\nvicinity of the surface front, will likely lead to a flash flood\npotential. CAMs also indicate a training potential as well. \n\nThere is also a low potential for severe thunderstorms today, ML\nCAPE is progged to range from 1500-2000 j/kg, though 0-6km \nshear should remain around 25-30kt. Model soundings indicate\nDCAPE values could reach 600-800 j/kg this afternoon, posing a\ndamaging wind potential should storm updrafts be sufficient for\ntaller storm cores. The Storm Predication Center has included\nmuch of the area east of the I-77 corridor in a Marginal Risk,\nor level 1 out of 5, for severe storms.\n\nConvection is expected to quickly diminish this evening with\nwaning instability, though a few showers will still be possible\novernight near the surface front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe surface boundary should continue to drop slowly south across\nthe region Saturday, reaching far southern PA, nrn WV, and\nsouthern OH by mid morning. The flow aloft is also progged to\nveer more to the NW on Saturday, driving the front south of the\narea by the end of the day. Until then, scattered showers and\nthunderstorms are possible with the front still in the area.\nthough chances should quickly diminish by late in the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nModel ensembles indicate a ridge should build eastward from the\nCentral CONUS through at least the middle of next week. This\nshould result in dry and gradually warmer weather through much\nof the period with rising 500 mb heights. Highs could again\nreach the lower 90s by Tuesday and Wednesday next week.",
